.Dd October 5, 2026
.Dt FUJICLI 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fujicli
.Nd manage Fujifilm cameras over USB
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
talks to Fujifilm cameras over USB using the Picture Transfer Protocol (PTP).
It lists and identifies cameras, reads and writes the film simulations stored
in the custom-setting slots, exports and imports full settings backups, and
renders RAW files with the camera's own image processor.
.Pp
The command-line interface is described in
.Xr fujicli 1 .
.Pp
Each camera is described in FML, a schema written in CUE under
.Pa fml/
in the source tree.
The schema lists the camera's USB identifiers, which features it supports, the
settings each feature touches, and the rules that constrain them.
The Rust code for every camera is generated from it at build time.
FML is described in
.Xr fujicli-fml 5 .
.Pp
The source tree is a Cargo workspace of five crates:
.Bl -tag -width "fujicodegen"
.It Sy fujicli
The command-line front end.
.It Sy fujicore
The library: USB and PTP transport, the feature traits, and the code generated
from FML.
.It Sy fujicodegen
Turns the exported FML into Rust.
Runs at build time only.
.It Sy ptp-cursor
The PTP wire codec:
.Vt PtpSerialize ,
.Vt PtpDeserialize
and PTP strings.
.It Sy ptp-macro
Derive macros for
.Vt PtpSerialize
and
.Vt PtpDeserialize .
.El
.Sh SUPPORTED CAMERAS
Every camera in the schema is recognised over USB, and
.Ql fujicli device info
should work on all of them.
The other features are enabled per camera:
.Bl -column "FUJIFILM X-S20" "X-Trans IV" "Backup" "Simulation" "Render" -offset indent
.It Sy Model Ta Sy Generation Ta Sy Backup Ta Sy Simulation Ta Sy Render
.It X-T5 Ta X-Trans V Ta yes Ta 7 slots Ta yes
.It X-S20 Ta X-Trans IV Ta yes Ta 4 slots Ta no
.El
.Pp
Both are tested.
The X-T5 is the only camera tested extensively, and the only one that renders.
.Pp
The remaining cameras are recognised but untested, and support
.Ql device info
only:
.Bl -tag -width "X-Trans III"
.It X-Trans
X-E1, X-M1.
.It X-Trans II
X70, X-E2, X-T1, X-T10.
.It X-Trans III
X100F, X-E3, X-H1, X-Pro2, X-T2, X-T20.
.It X-Trans IV
X100V, X-E4, X-Pro3, X-S10, X-T3, X-T4.
.It X-Trans V
X100VI, X-H2, X-H2S.
.El
.Pp
To report that a feature works on a camera, open an issue with the model, the
firmware version, the USB identifier printed by
.Ql fujicli device list ,
and the output of the command run with
.Fl vvv .
For rendering, attach a small sample and its rendered output.
.Ss Emulation
.Fl -emulate Ar vendor : Ns Ar product
makes
.Nm
treat the connected camera as the model with that USB identifier.
It selects which generated implementation is used and nothing else.
It does not add support for a camera missing from the schema.
.Pp
Emulation exists for development and reverse engineering.
The emulated model's property codes, value ranges and slot count may not match
the real camera, so reads can return garbage and writes can store values the
camera does not expect.
Rendering under emulation is unpredictable, and its output should not be
trusted.
Take a backup first.
.Sh HOW IT WORKS
.Ss Transport
The camera appears as a PTP device on USB.
.Nm
claims its interface, opens a PTP session, and exchanges command, data and
response containers over the bulk endpoints.
Large transfers are split into chunks of the camera's
.Va chunk_size .
Most of what follows uses Fujifilm's vendor extensions to PTP: vendor device
properties, and vendor operations for sending objects.
.Ss Simulations
A film simulation is the set of image settings stored in one custom-setting
slot, C1 to C7 on the X-T5.
Each setting is a separate device property.
.Pp
To read a slot,
.Nm
writes the slot number to the custom-setting property, then reads each setting
property in turn.
The settings are read in a dependency order.
Some settings are only meaningful when others have particular values: white
balance temperature only when white balance is set to temperature, for
example.
Such a setting is gated: it is read only after the settings it depends on, and
only if they allow it.
Otherwise it is left absent.
Both the order and the gates are derived from the schema's rules.
.Pp
To update a slot,
.Nm
reads the slot, merges the requested changes over it, repairs the result
against the rules, and writes back every setting in the same order.
Absent settings are not written.
A setting whose option is marked flaky is skipped, with a warning, if the
camera rejects it.
.Pp
Rules have a severity.
An
.Sy error
rule that the merged settings violate is repaired when possible: a setting the
request did not change is cleared or set to a value that satisfies the rule.
Settings named in the request are never changed by repair.
If no repair works, the update fails with the rule's message and nothing is
written.
.Sy warning
and
.Sy info
rules are only logged.
.Pp
Exported simulations are JSON and are tied to the camera model.
.Ss Backups
A backup is the camera's complete settings dump, transferred as a PTP object.
.Nm
stores it as an opaque blob and sends it back unchanged on import.
.Ss Rendering
Rendering runs the camera's RAW converter on a RAF file, as Fujifilm X RAW
Studio does:
.Bl -enum
.It
The RAF is sent to the camera.
.It
The camera's conversion profile for that image is read.
It holds the settings the image was shot with, encoded as a fixed list of
32-bit fields.
.It
The user's partial profile is merged over it.
The partial is built from a simulation, if one was given, overlaid with any
individual settings.
A simulation can come from a slot, an exported file, or another image's maker
notes.
.It
The result is repaired against the camera's render rules.
Render rules can also refer to the profile as shot.
The camera cannot raise the dynamic range above the value it was shot at, or
enable a teleconverter crop that was off at the time, and rules of this kind
use the
.Sy original
scope to express that.
Values as shot are never changed by repair.
.It
The repaired profile is written back.
.It
The conversion is started, and the result is fetched from the camera and
deleted there.
.El
.Pp
Reading a simulation from an image uses
.Xr exiftool 1
to extract the maker notes, so it must be installed.
.Sh ADDING A CAMERA
A camera is an entry in
.Pa fml/camera.cue .
An entry with a name, a generation and a USB product identifier is enough for
.Ql device list
and
.Ql device info .
Each further feature is enabled by declaring it in the entry's
.Va features
block, after it has been verified on a real camera.
The syntax is in
.Xr fujicli-fml 5 .
.Bl -enum
.It
Take a backup of the camera, with
.Ql fujicli device reverse backup export
if the camera does not declare backups yet.
.It
Add the entry.
Its key is the camera's identifier, and must be a valid Rust identifier once
converted.
Set
.Va usb.chunk_size
if the camera accepts transfers larger than 1 MiB.
.It
Build with
.Ql cargo build .
The build exports the schema with CUE, which reports any error in the entry.
.It
Probe each feature as described in
.Sx REVERSE ENGINEERING ,
and declare the ones that work:
.Bl -tag -width "simulation"
.It Va backup
Set to
.Sy true
once a backup round-trips.
The exchange has been the same on every camera seen so far.
.It Va simulation
The number of slots, the settings, and the rules.
Most cameras use their generation's settings and rules.
A setting the generation lacks is added to the list, and a new option is
declared in
.Pa fml/option.cue
first.
Check that
.Ql fujicli simulation get
and
.Ql fujicli simulation set
round-trip on every slot.
.It Va render
The profile code, the profile's fields in wire order, the transformations
between user-facing values and wire values, and the render rules.
.El
.It
Pin the values the camera accepts with rules on the camera, even where an
option's global definition already allows them.
A value later added to an option for a newer camera must not silently become
valid on an older one.
.It
Update
.Sx SUPPORTED CAMERAS
in this page.
Mark only features you have verified yourself.
.El
.Sh REVERSE ENGINEERING
The hidden
.Ql fujicli device reverse
commands probe a camera regardless of what its schema entry declares.
Run them with
.Fl vvv
to log raw PTP traffic.
They can leave a camera in a bad state: take a full backup first.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql fujicli device reverse info
Dumps the raw reply to
.Dv GetDeviceInfo .
If it succeeds, the camera speaks PTP and
.Ql device info
will work.
.It Ql fujicli device reverse backup export Ar file
.It Ql fujicli device reverse backup import Ar file
Exports and imports a backup.
A successful round trip means
.Va backup
can be declared.
.It Ql fujicli device reverse simulation
Reads every known simulation property on every slot.
Errors on individual properties are expected and show which settings the
camera lacks.
It reads only, and does not check which values the camera accepts.
.El
.Ss Render profiles
The conversion profile format is undocumented.
It is recovered by capturing the USB traffic between the camera and Fujifilm X
RAW Studio.
.Pp
The profile is serialised as a 16-bit field count, the profile code as a
string, 0x1EE bytes of padding, and one 32-bit value per field.
The padding is hard-coded in
.Pa crates/fujicodegen/src/common/renders/camera.rs
until a second camera is reversed.
.Pp
The setup is a Windows virtual machine running X RAW Studio, with the camera
passed through to it over USB, and Wireshark on the host.
.Ql support/monitor-rendering.sh
needs a
.Xr tshark 1
patched to expose
.Va frame.raw
for USB traffic.
.Bl -enum
.It
Load the
.Sy usbmon
kernel module.
.It
Start the script with
.Ev INTERFACE
set to the
.Sy usbmon
interface of the camera's bus, before connecting the camera:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ INTERFACE=usbmon1 ./support/monitor-rendering.sh
.Ed
.It
Start the virtual machine, connect the camera and pass it through.
.It
Open X RAW Studio and change settings.
The live preview is enough; the image does not need to be saved.
.It
The script prints each conversion profile the host sends.
Compare successive profiles to map each field to a setting.
.El
.Pp
Each field becomes an entry in the camera's
.Va render.fields ,
in wire order.
A field with no matching option gets an inline identifier.
When one user-facing value is stored as several wire values, as HDR800+ is
stored as HDR800 plus a priority flag, declare a transformation.
Transformations with a single equality condition and a unique result are
inverted when reading, so the user sees the original value.
.Pp
X RAW Studio sometimes reads fewer fields than it writes.
Treat what it writes as authoritative.
.Sh ARCHITECTURE
.Ss Build
.Pa crates/fujicore/build.rs
runs
.Ql cue export ./fml --out json
and passes the result to
.Fn fujicodegen::generate ,
which writes
.Pa crates/fujicore/src/generated/ :
.Bl -tag -width "simulations.rs"
.It Pa options.rs
One type per option, with its wire encoding, parsing, and serialisation.
.It Pa cameras.rs
One type per camera, the features it implements, and the registry used to
match USB identifiers.
.It Pa simulations.rs
Simulation types and rules.
.It Pa renders.rs
Render profile types, rules, and the profile codec.
.It Pa cli.rs
The command-line arguments for simulation and render settings.
.El
.Pp
The directory is not tracked by git.
Unchanged files are left alone and stale files are removed.
Edits to them are lost on the next build.
.Pp
Within
.Sy fujicodegen ,
.Pa ast/
holds the parsed schema,
.Pa schema/
the analyses, and
.Pa common/
and
.Pa cli/
the emitters.
.Ss Generated types
Every option becomes a Rust type implementing
.Vt Default ,
.Vt Eq ,
.Vt Ord
and
.Vt Hash .
.Pp
For each camera with simulations, and again for each with rendering, there are
two types.
The draft type,
.Vt XT5SimulationDraft
or
.Vt XT5RenderProfileDraft ,
has every field optional and is used while merging and repairing.
The strict type,
.Vt XT5Simulation
or
.Vt XT5RenderProfile ,
is what is read from and written to the camera.
Only its gated fields are optional.
.Vt SimulationBase
and
.Vt RenderBase
are the union of all cameras' fields, and carry requests from the command line.
.Pp
Each camera's rules become a module,
.Ql x_t5 ,
with:
.Bl -tag -width "apply_transformations"
.It Fn apply_transformations
Rewrites user-facing values into wire values.
.It Fn solve
Checks the error rules and repairs violations.
.It Fn emit_warnings_and_infos
Logs the warning and info rules that hold.
.It Fn try_update_from
Merges a partial draft into the current one, and runs the three above.
.El
.Pp
The runtime only sees traits.
.Vt CameraBase
has one accessor per feature, returning nothing by default.
The generated camera types override those they declare.
.Ss Analyses
The analyses in
.Pa crates/fujicodegen/src/schema/
turn rules into code.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Normalisation
Every rule condition is rewritten in disjunctive normal form: an
.Sy or
of
.Sy and Ns s
of comparisons, with negation pushed into the comparisons.
The later passes all work on this form.
.It Alias substitution
Rules are written against user-facing values, but checked after
transformations have run.
Each transformation's condition is replaced in the rules by what it produces,
so a rule about HDR800+ applies to HDR800 with the priority flag set.
.It Expansion exemption
The dual of substitution.
A rule that never mentions an alias value can still match what the alias
produces: a rule forbidding a dynamic range while priority is on would reject
HDR800+.
Such rules are given an exception for the exact output of the transformation.
.It Presence
A rule of the form
.Do
.Va X
is set and
.Va Y
is not suitable
.Dc
means
.Va X
should only be read when
.Va Y
allows it.
From such rules the pass builds a graph of the settings, ordered so that
.Va Y
comes before
.Va X ,
and the gate for each setting.
Settings are read and written in the topological order closest to their
declaration order.
A cycle is a build error.
.It Repair
For each violated error rule, try changing one setting the request did not
name: clear it, if it is gated, or set it to the value the rule requires.
A change is kept only if no rule that held before now fails.
Values as shot are never changed.
.It Inverses
Transformations with a single equality condition and a result no other
transformation produces are inverted on read, in reverse order.
Others are skipped with a build warning.
Reading and writing a profile is therefore equivalent, but not necessarily
identical byte for byte.
.El
